Package: dhcpig
Architecture: all
Depends: python3-scapy (>= 2.5),
         ${misc:Depends},
         ${python3:Depends}
Suggests: pkexec
Description: DHCP exhaustion and network-hardening validation tool
 DHCPig checks whether a network defends itself against DHCP-layer attacks.
 It exhausts a DHCP pool, releases and re-acquires neighbouring clients'
 leases by name, contests addresses it has taken with forged RFC 5227 ARP
 conflicts, and fingerprints hosts from their DHCP options and MAC vendor.
 Each run ends in an evidence-backed verdict describing what the network
 allowed, so it can be used to show whether controls such as DHCP snooping,
 port security and Dynamic ARP Inspection are effective.
 .
 Both a command line tool (dhcpig) and a local web interface (dhcpig-web)
 are provided; they drive the same engine. The web interface binds to the
 loopback address, requires a token printed at startup, and runs only when
 invoked explicitly - no daemon or system service is registered.
 .
 Read-only modes (scan, active-scan) and a reconnaissance mode (--dry-run)
 are available. The disruptive modes are separate, explicitly named
 subcommands, and leases taken can be handed back with "dhcpig restore" or,
 once the process has exited, "dhcpig release-previous". Raw socket access
 (root) is required. Only use this against networks you own or are
 explicitly authorised to test.
